1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,440
And yet, of course, this is a policy

2
00:00:02,440 --> 00:00:05,040
that is very hard to get adopted.

3
00:00:05,040 --> 00:00:07,000
People are drag kicking and screaming.

4
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:09,160
Even in Singapore, where they've used this policy

5
00:00:09,160 --> 00:00:12,880
to keep roads running smoothly at all hours a day.

6
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:14,760
Still, there's a YouTube video of people

7
00:00:14,760 --> 00:00:16,920
denouncing the horrors of electronic road pricing

8
00:00:16,920 --> 00:00:18,720
and how angry they are.

9
00:00:18,720 --> 00:00:19,220
Yes?

10
00:00:19,220 --> 00:00:20,920
They do something similar like that on the Beltway,

11
00:00:20,920 --> 00:00:21,400
don't they?

12
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:22,120
Isn't there a very close?

13
00:00:22,120 --> 00:00:23,960
So now there are high occupancy toll lanes.

14
00:00:23,960 --> 00:00:25,960
Although the way they did it was they

15
00:00:25,960 --> 00:00:28,240
added lanes that people didn't have before to get over

16
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:29,880
the status quo bias.

17
00:00:29,880 --> 00:00:32,200
And you can still go and drive in the regular lanes

18
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:34,880
if you want to, but it's just these new lanes.

19
00:00:34,880 --> 00:00:37,160
These ones, either you have to pay a toll

20
00:00:37,160 --> 00:00:39,680
or you have to have a high occupancy vehicle.

21
00:00:39,680 --> 00:00:41,560
So understand, there is going to be something

22
00:00:41,560 --> 00:00:42,720
happening on Route 66.

23
00:00:42,720 --> 00:00:45,600
Although I'm not sure whether it's the same thing where

24
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:47,240
you're just putting tolls on new lanes

25
00:00:47,240 --> 00:00:49,400
or whether they're actually going to start charging

26
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:50,560
for existing lanes.

27
00:00:50,560 --> 00:00:52,920
Does anyone know the details?

28
00:00:52,920 --> 00:00:54,680
But in any case, the problem, again,

29
00:00:54,680 --> 00:00:57,920
even if in the next 30 years, every road in America

30
00:00:57,920 --> 00:01:01,800
gets congestion pricing, there's still the question,

31
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:06,880
well, it took 100 years of traffic for this to happen.

32
00:01:06,880 --> 00:01:09,400
And if you ask normal people, the answer is pretty clear.

33
00:01:09,400 --> 00:01:11,320
They just really resent it.

34
00:01:11,320 --> 00:01:12,320
This is terrible.

35
00:01:12,320 --> 00:01:13,800
I shouldn't have to pay for this.

36
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:15,520
This ought to be free.

37
00:01:15,520 --> 00:01:18,720
Look, either you're paying in time or money.

38
00:01:18,720 --> 00:01:23,080
And the value of the time seems so much greater than the money

39
00:01:23,080 --> 00:01:23,580
is.

40
00:01:23,580 --> 00:01:24,080
It's crazy.

41
00:01:24,080 --> 00:01:26,720
But it's a normal reaction.

42
00:01:26,720 --> 00:01:29,400
All right, another example, anti-foreign bias.

43
00:01:29,400 --> 00:01:31,320
So it's one thing to convince people

44
00:01:31,320 --> 00:01:33,000
that when one American buys something

45
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,680
from another American, they're probably both better off.

46
00:01:35,680 --> 00:01:38,160
Like, fine, fine.

47
00:01:38,160 --> 00:01:41,160
But what if an American buys something from a Chinese?

48
00:01:41,160 --> 00:01:44,400
This is where people's spider sense starts tingling.

49
00:01:44,400 --> 00:01:46,200
No, wait a second here.

50
00:01:46,200 --> 00:01:49,080
Wait, you're telling me there's another country called China.

51
00:01:49,080 --> 00:01:50,960
They're selling us this stuff.

52
00:01:50,960 --> 00:01:54,200
Oh my god, what are they planning on doing?

53
00:01:54,200 --> 00:01:56,080
What's in it for them?

54
00:01:56,080 --> 00:01:59,360
This is a reaction you can see from even

55
00:01:59,360 --> 00:02:01,080
for the interactive economics students

56
00:02:01,080 --> 00:02:02,400
when you start talking about it.

57
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:04,440
It's like, excuse me, professor.

58
00:02:04,440 --> 00:02:06,520
I think I know what's wrong here.

59
00:02:06,520 --> 00:02:09,280
You realize the Chinese are selling us all this cheap stuff

60
00:02:09,280 --> 00:02:11,040
and it's destroying American industry

61
00:02:11,040 --> 00:02:13,040
and getting very, very angry.

62
00:02:13,040 --> 00:02:16,760
It's like, look, I'm just trying to take a roll here.

63
00:02:16,760 --> 00:02:17,920
Talk about this.

64
00:02:17,920 --> 00:02:19,840
We'll get to that in a bit.

65
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:21,340
But you don't understand, professor.

66
00:02:21,340 --> 00:02:23,280
There's a place called China, which

67
00:02:23,280 --> 00:02:25,520
is a different country from the United States.

68
00:02:25,520 --> 00:02:26,480
You get this.

69
00:02:26,480 --> 00:02:28,000
I get all of that.

70
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:29,240
Doesn't really change.

71
00:02:29,240 --> 00:02:30,280
Maybe it doesn't matter.

72
00:02:30,280 --> 00:02:31,760
How could it not matter?

73
00:02:31,760 --> 00:02:33,840
Well, let me tell you a parable.

74
00:02:33,840 --> 00:02:38,000
It's called the parable of the Iowa corn crop.

75
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:40,720
In this parable, a parable is a story

76
00:02:40,720 --> 00:02:43,680
that is a symbol for other things.

77
00:02:43,680 --> 00:02:44,320
Or an allegory.

78
00:02:44,320 --> 00:02:45,360
This is also an allegory.

79
00:02:45,360 --> 00:02:48,360
So in this story, there's a scientist

80
00:02:48,360 --> 00:02:52,960
who says I have found a new way to turn corn into cars.

81
00:02:52,960 --> 00:02:54,120
And everyone laughs at him.

82
00:02:54,120 --> 00:02:57,120
It's scientifically, molecularly impossible.

83
00:02:57,120 --> 00:02:59,160
But the scientist goes and builds his factory over

84
00:02:59,160 --> 00:03:00,640
by the abandoned docks.

85
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:03,120
And amazing, corn goes in.

86
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:04,400
Cars come out.

87
00:03:04,400 --> 00:03:06,000
Everyone's like, this guy's a genius.

88
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:10,720
He has figured out a way to transmute corn into cars.

89
00:03:10,720 --> 00:03:15,320
He's developed some kind of incredible Star Trek

90
00:03:15,320 --> 00:03:17,240
Transformer array or something.

91
00:03:17,240 --> 00:03:20,120
Until finally, a journalist sneaks from the factory

92
00:03:20,120 --> 00:03:22,280
and finds out it's just a dock.

93
00:03:22,280 --> 00:03:24,400
And all that happens is Japanese cars come in,

94
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:26,640
or ships come in with cars.

95
00:03:26,640 --> 00:03:28,520
And American trucks with corn go in.

96
00:03:28,520 --> 00:03:30,040
And they swap the corn in the cars.

97
00:03:30,040 --> 00:03:31,160
And they move out.

98
00:03:31,160 --> 00:03:34,160
And then the journalist writes a story, corn into cars a fraud,

99
00:03:34,160 --> 00:03:35,160
a lie.

100
00:03:35,160 --> 00:03:37,660
And the guy gets arrested for violating all the international

101
00:03:37,660 --> 00:03:38,200
trade laws.

102
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:39,800
The end.

103
00:03:39,800 --> 00:03:43,280
And the moral of the story, of course, is the scientist.

104
00:03:43,280 --> 00:03:45,840
Did the scientist have a technology

105
00:03:45,840 --> 00:03:47,920
for turning corn into cars?

106
00:03:47,920 --> 00:03:50,120
Of course he did.

107
00:03:50,120 --> 00:03:52,600
Trade is the magical technology that

108
00:03:52,600 --> 00:03:55,320
can turn anything into anything.

109
00:03:55,320 --> 00:03:57,080
The fact that a country called Japan even

110
00:03:57,080 --> 00:03:58,880
exists with people in it is completely

111
00:03:58,880 --> 00:04:01,520
irrelevant from the American point of view.

112
00:04:01,520 --> 00:04:04,600
It might as just as well have been a big vat where

113
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:06,240
you throw in corn and cars come out,

114
00:04:06,240 --> 00:04:08,160
as far as Americans are concerned.

115
00:04:08,160 --> 00:04:11,400
It's the same thing, where it's just a technology

116
00:04:11,400 --> 00:04:13,600
for taking stuff we have and turning it into something

117
00:04:13,600 --> 00:04:15,040
that we want more.

118
00:04:15,040 --> 00:04:17,480
And yet, very hard for people to accept

119
00:04:17,480 --> 00:04:20,520
this way of looking at it.

120
00:04:20,520 --> 00:04:22,280
And of course, from a human point of view,

121
00:04:22,280 --> 00:04:25,280
trade is better than just a technology.

122
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:29,480
Because with trade, there's a whole other country of people

123
00:04:29,480 --> 00:04:31,360
who are benefiting also.

124
00:04:31,360 --> 00:04:33,880
Whereas just for a machine, where you pour in the corn,

125
00:04:33,880 --> 00:04:36,400
turn the switch, and we burn up the corn and get a car,

126
00:04:36,400 --> 00:04:37,160
it's good for us.

127
00:04:37,160 --> 00:04:38,920
But there's no one else benefiting.

128
00:04:38,920 --> 00:04:41,280
But this is actually where the corn goes someplace else.

129
00:04:41,280 --> 00:04:43,320
And someone else gets to enjoy the stuff

130
00:04:43,320 --> 00:04:46,080
that, if it were just a machine, would have been burned up

131
00:04:46,080 --> 00:04:48,240
in the process.

132
00:04:48,240 --> 00:04:49,740
And of course, when you're thinking

133
00:04:49,740 --> 00:04:52,240
about what kinds of policies would any foreign buyers

134
00:04:52,240 --> 00:04:55,320
explain, well, all the policies designed

135
00:04:55,320 --> 00:04:59,720
to keep out foreign products fit very nicely here.

136
00:04:59,720 --> 00:05:02,000
Every time someone complains about countries selling us

137
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:05,400
cheap products, Bastiaan had a very simple thought experiment,

138
00:05:05,400 --> 00:05:07,160
which has never really been answered.

139
00:05:07,160 --> 00:05:10,040
And he said, look, suppose that countries gave us

140
00:05:10,040 --> 00:05:12,600
everything they made for free forever.

141
00:05:12,600 --> 00:05:14,400
Would that be bad for us?

142
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:16,780
Suppose they just worked all day, and whatever they made,

143
00:05:16,780 --> 00:05:17,960
they gave us for free.

144
00:05:17,960 --> 00:05:18,760
Be great.

145
00:05:18,760 --> 00:05:20,560
Yes, wouldn't that be great?

146
00:05:20,560 --> 00:05:22,920
All right, well, cheap stuff is somewhere

147
00:05:22,920 --> 00:05:25,480
in between expensive stuff and free stuff.

148
00:05:25,480 --> 00:05:27,400
It's not as good as free, but it's

149
00:05:27,400 --> 00:05:30,400
better than charging the normal price we're used to.

150
00:05:30,400 --> 00:05:34,520
And the closer we get to free, the better.

151
00:05:34,520 --> 00:05:38,080
Of course, sometimes there is a back story of, for step one,

152
00:05:38,080 --> 00:05:40,480
we go and we destroy their industry.

153
00:05:40,480 --> 00:05:42,640
Then we jack up the price.

154
00:05:42,640 --> 00:05:44,760
All right, kind of like the story of, well,

155
00:05:44,760 --> 00:05:48,280
Starbucks is just about ready to engage in phase two,

156
00:05:48,280 --> 00:05:50,600
where now that they've destroyed all the other coffee,

157
00:05:50,600 --> 00:05:53,160
then they're going to start charging $50 a cup.

158
00:05:53,160 --> 00:05:55,000
And what choice will we have?

159
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:58,560
We'll either have to pay it or die.

160
00:05:58,560 --> 00:06:00,680
Fall asleep at our desks anyway.

161
00:06:00,680 --> 00:06:03,760
All right, but so phase two does not seem to happen.

162
00:06:03,760 --> 00:06:07,120
I remember, actually, back before Amazon,

163
00:06:07,120 --> 00:06:09,280
when Borders and Barnes & Noble superstores

164
00:06:09,280 --> 00:06:11,280
were sweeping the book market, there

165
00:06:11,280 --> 00:06:13,120
were people saying, you naive fools who

166
00:06:13,120 --> 00:06:15,560
think that this is just a great thing for the consumer.

167
00:06:15,560 --> 00:06:17,140
Once all those other stores are gone,

168
00:06:17,140 --> 00:06:20,240
then you'll see what Borders and Barnes & Noble do.

169
00:06:20,240 --> 00:06:23,960
Right, yeah, they go out of business.

170
00:06:23,960 --> 00:06:25,840
But of course, these people imagine

171
00:06:25,840 --> 00:06:26,960
they're more sophisticated.

172
00:06:26,960 --> 00:06:31,480
And this is phase one of a plan to destroy all the competition

173
00:06:31,480 --> 00:06:34,080
and then jack up the price to astronomical levels

174
00:06:34,080 --> 00:06:36,240
when we have no other place to buy our books,

175
00:06:36,240 --> 00:06:38,880
rather than the obvious thing of, they're selling us cheap stuff

176
00:06:38,880 --> 00:06:42,240
and it's likely to continue or get better.

177
00:06:42,240 --> 00:06:45,120
All right, so and then, of course,

178
00:06:45,120 --> 00:06:47,800
immigration restrictions, one of my pet peeves,

179
00:06:47,800 --> 00:06:51,520
explained very well by a deformed bias.

180
00:06:51,520 --> 00:06:54,000
Probably the strangest argument against immigration

181
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:55,560
that I've heard, and people, listen,

182
00:06:55,560 --> 00:06:56,600
there's one of the arguments people tell me,

183
00:06:56,600 --> 00:06:59,080
is they're like, Brian, you know what you don't understand?

184
00:06:59,080 --> 00:07:00,880
All right, you have my attention.

185
00:07:00,880 --> 00:07:01,920
What don't I understand?

186
00:07:01,920 --> 00:07:04,600
You don't understand, look, immigrants aren't just hands

187
00:07:04,600 --> 00:07:05,800
to do a job.

188
00:07:05,800 --> 00:07:08,760
They're human beings.

189
00:07:08,760 --> 00:07:12,640
Right, first of all, I knew that.

190
00:07:12,640 --> 00:07:15,640
Second of all, that seems to be an extra argument

191
00:07:15,640 --> 00:07:17,240
in favor of immigration.

192
00:07:17,240 --> 00:07:19,920
If it were just robots that we were letting in,

193
00:07:19,920 --> 00:07:22,480
that we were impoverishing ourselves by not letting in,

194
00:07:22,480 --> 00:07:24,680
I said, well, at least the robots don't feel pain.

195
00:07:24,680 --> 00:07:25,880
Or that's my theory, anyway.

196
00:07:25,880 --> 00:07:28,280
Robin might disagree.

197
00:07:28,280 --> 00:07:30,000
Robots don't have any feelings.

198
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:32,280
They don't feel sad to see their kids hungry.

199
00:07:32,280 --> 00:07:33,600
So I guess it's not that bad.

200
00:07:33,600 --> 00:07:35,520
But in fact, it's a human being.

201
00:07:35,520 --> 00:07:38,600
And we seem to be an extra reason to want them here.

202
00:07:38,600 --> 00:07:40,880
Well, but you understand, human beings do bad things.

203
00:07:40,880 --> 00:07:43,160
Oh, that's the thing I didn't understand.

204
00:07:43,160 --> 00:07:45,440
Well, they also do good things.

205
00:07:45,440 --> 00:07:49,520
And on average, when we just look around the world,

206
00:07:49,520 --> 00:07:52,360
seems like human beings must, on balance, be a good thing.

207
00:07:52,360 --> 00:07:54,040
Because otherwise, the world would

208
00:07:54,040 --> 00:07:56,560
be worse with people in it.

209
00:07:56,560 --> 00:07:58,720
How does civilization exist if the average person

210
00:07:58,720 --> 00:08:01,680
is worse than nothing?

211
00:08:01,680 --> 00:08:03,000
That's my question, at least.

212
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:08,240
Seems like normally a person would be a good thing.

213
00:08:08,240 --> 00:08:10,600
And then my kinds of questions are more like, well,

214
00:08:10,600 --> 00:08:12,600
if your janitor suddenly drops dead,

215
00:08:12,600 --> 00:08:16,240
do you celebrate that the average worker quality has

216
00:08:16,240 --> 00:08:17,200
risen?

217
00:08:17,200 --> 00:08:21,360
Is that your reaction when the janitor dies?

218
00:08:21,360 --> 00:08:22,800
No.

219
00:08:22,800 --> 00:08:25,640
And could it be that even someone

220
00:08:25,640 --> 00:08:27,520
who is not the smartest person in the world

221
00:08:27,520 --> 00:08:30,520
still is better than nothing?

222
00:08:30,520 --> 00:08:32,840
Maybe even he's a wonderful thing.

223
00:08:32,840 --> 00:08:33,800
He enjoys life.

224
00:08:33,800 --> 00:08:34,600
He contributes.

225
00:08:34,600 --> 00:08:36,480
What's the problem?

226
00:08:36,480 --> 00:08:39,920
So anyway, that is my reaction.

227
00:08:39,920 --> 00:08:42,040
All right, many others disagree.

228
00:08:42,040 --> 00:08:43,520
All right, I'm writing a comic book,

229
00:08:43,520 --> 00:08:46,120
which will change all their minds.

230
00:08:46,120 --> 00:08:48,120
All right, then, your next figure,

231
00:08:48,120 --> 00:08:49,880
talking about network bias.

232
00:08:49,880 --> 00:08:52,640
General tendency to evaluate economic performance

233
00:08:52,640 --> 00:08:55,200
based upon employment rather than production.

234
00:08:55,200 --> 00:08:57,360
Very easy to make, especially during recession.

235
00:08:57,360 --> 00:09:02,080
Bodygate, if you just think about it

236
00:09:02,080 --> 00:09:04,480
in terms of basic principles that say, well,

237
00:09:04,480 --> 00:09:06,920
was it bad that the tractor was developed?

238
00:09:06,920 --> 00:09:10,360
Was it bad that all the labor saving agriculture machinery

239
00:09:10,360 --> 00:09:15,720
came along, which did indeed destroy vast numbers of jobs?

240
00:09:15,720 --> 00:09:17,200
Sure seems like it was a good thing

241
00:09:17,200 --> 00:09:18,720
that this happened because it allowed

242
00:09:18,720 --> 00:09:21,000
one person to feed a vast number of people

243
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:23,080
so we could go and do other things.

244
00:09:23,080 --> 00:09:24,960
Now, when this technology was coming along,

245
00:09:24,960 --> 00:09:28,440
if you had just said, look, this is wonderful, and don't worry.

246
00:09:28,440 --> 00:09:29,880
All the people being put out of work

247
00:09:29,880 --> 00:09:31,760
will just do something else.

248
00:09:31,760 --> 00:09:35,600
In 1840, if you had said, well, they'll just do something else,

249
00:09:35,600 --> 00:09:38,640
this would have sounded like the most naive, obtuse thing

250
00:09:38,640 --> 00:09:40,680
a human being has ever said.

251
00:09:40,680 --> 00:09:45,200
Because, look, agriculture is what 95% of human beings

252
00:09:45,200 --> 00:09:47,840
have done for the last 10,000 years.

253
00:09:47,840 --> 00:09:50,040
And you think that we can eliminate almost all

254
00:09:50,040 --> 00:09:53,600
this employment and people will just find something else.

255
00:09:53,600 --> 00:09:56,640
But you refuse to identify or give any specifics on,

256
00:09:56,640 --> 00:09:58,600
and that's what will happen.

257
00:09:58,600 --> 00:10:00,560
And yet, of course, that naive, obtuse thing

258
00:10:00,560 --> 00:10:02,600
was sensible advice.

259
00:10:02,600 --> 00:10:03,760
And there is a simple story.

260
00:10:03,760 --> 00:10:07,080
Why was it that in 1840, it's hard to give specifics

261
00:10:07,080 --> 00:10:09,440
about what all of the non-farmers

262
00:10:09,440 --> 00:10:10,840
are going to do with their lives?

263
00:10:13,880 --> 00:10:15,480
We've never been there before.

264
00:10:15,480 --> 00:10:17,160
Like, you're going to say, well, they'll

265
00:10:17,160 --> 00:10:19,040
invent an internet of some kind.

266
00:10:19,040 --> 00:10:21,160
Say, well, there'll be an Industrial Revolution.

267
00:10:21,160 --> 00:10:22,720
It's like, well, there's some industry.

268
00:10:22,720 --> 00:10:23,280
Oh, come on.

269
00:10:23,280 --> 00:10:25,800
Well, look, everyone's going to work with trains.

270
00:10:25,800 --> 00:10:27,760
Like, everyone's going to build trains all day.

271
00:10:27,760 --> 00:10:28,600
That can't be.

272
00:10:28,600 --> 00:10:29,800
What, steamships?

273
00:10:29,800 --> 00:10:32,960
There's only so many steamships we need, sir.

274
00:10:32,960 --> 00:10:34,280
All right, now, cotton gins?

275
00:10:34,280 --> 00:10:34,800
All right.

276
00:10:34,800 --> 00:10:37,520
And then, like, pretty soon, the well is going to dry.

277
00:10:37,520 --> 00:10:40,480
And it's hard to name any other specific things people will do.

278
00:10:40,480 --> 00:10:42,240
Say, look, labor's valuable.

279
00:10:42,240 --> 00:10:44,280
There's always unmet wants.

280
00:10:44,280 --> 00:10:46,800
We'll figure out something or other to do.

281
00:10:46,800 --> 00:10:50,200
Very dissatisfying, yet true.

282
00:10:50,200 --> 00:10:52,800
So a bit hard to convince people of.

283
00:10:52,800 --> 00:10:55,160
And there's always the this time it's different.

284
00:10:55,160 --> 00:10:57,760
So yes, this time, automation will, in fact,

285
00:10:57,760 --> 00:11:00,600
disemploy the workforce.

286
00:11:00,600 --> 00:11:02,640
If we're able to get rid of agriculture

287
00:11:02,640 --> 00:11:05,960
as a source of employment for virtually all purposes,

288
00:11:05,960 --> 00:11:08,000
but we can't get rid of the other stuff we've got

289
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:11,320
and find something else to do, very hard to understand why.

290
00:11:11,320 --> 00:11:13,840
Like, if we get rid of the thing that almost everyone's done

291
00:11:13,840 --> 00:11:15,360
for almost all of history, why can't we

292
00:11:15,360 --> 00:11:17,060
get rid of the stuff that we're doing now

293
00:11:17,060 --> 00:11:20,280
and still find something else to do?

294
00:11:20,280 --> 00:11:23,440
Hard to understand what that would be.

295
00:11:23,440 --> 00:11:25,200
All right, and by the way, so if you

296
00:11:25,200 --> 00:11:26,640
know your history of the 1930s, it

297
00:11:26,640 --> 00:11:28,600
was extremely common in the 30s to say

298
00:11:28,600 --> 00:11:32,080
that this is technological unemployment.

299
00:11:32,080 --> 00:11:34,080
It was possibly the most popular view, actually.

300
00:11:34,080 --> 00:11:37,560
So Keynesianism made perfect sense by comparison.

301
00:11:37,560 --> 00:11:39,280
At least they were saying, look, there's

302
00:11:39,280 --> 00:11:42,080
no reason why we couldn't put everybody back to work.

303
00:11:42,080 --> 00:11:43,360
There's still plenty of stuff.

304
00:11:43,360 --> 00:11:45,560
There's plenty of unmet wants.

305
00:11:45,560 --> 00:11:47,680
And yet, plenty of people in the 30s were just saying, look,

306
00:11:47,680 --> 00:11:49,640
well, this is the end of the line here.

307
00:11:49,640 --> 00:11:52,040
There's nothing left to do.

308
00:11:52,040 --> 00:11:54,040
Let's think about how, if you went back in time,

309
00:11:54,040 --> 00:11:55,760
how ridiculous that would seem.

310
00:11:55,760 --> 00:11:58,360
There's nothing left to do.

311
00:11:58,360 --> 00:11:59,920
I could just think of tons of stuff.

312
00:11:59,920 --> 00:12:01,760
But we have the advantage of hindsight.

313
00:12:01,760 --> 00:12:03,720
We know what has been done since then.

314
00:12:03,720 --> 00:12:05,840
But you go and name stuff that is going to happen

315
00:12:05,840 --> 00:12:08,880
in the future, very not.

316
00:12:08,880 --> 00:12:10,560
And again, of course, make work bias

317
00:12:10,560 --> 00:12:13,360
is an obvious explanation for the many efforts

318
00:12:13,360 --> 00:12:16,520
to go and save jobs by law, efforts

319
00:12:16,520 --> 00:12:19,200
to prevent people from ever being fired or laid off,

320
00:12:19,200 --> 00:12:22,160
of course, efforts to go and try to make sure

321
00:12:22,160 --> 00:12:27,560
that we sell our countries as much as we possibly

322
00:12:27,560 --> 00:12:30,400
can for as few imports as we can possibly get.

323
00:12:30,400 --> 00:12:33,320
That way, we are able to conserve our jobs.

324
00:12:33,320 --> 00:12:36,480
So any time people think about trying to stamp out Uber

325
00:12:36,480 --> 00:12:38,640
because it's hurting taxi employment, well,

326
00:12:38,640 --> 00:12:39,360
that one's weird.

327
00:12:39,360 --> 00:12:40,920
It sure seems like there's just more people employed

328
00:12:40,920 --> 00:12:42,200
in driving as a result of that.

329
00:12:42,200 --> 00:12:44,320
Anyone actually know the numbers?

330
00:12:44,320 --> 00:12:47,400
It seems like there's so, like, demand elasticity for driving

331
00:12:47,400 --> 00:12:49,920
services is so high, it actually seems

332
00:12:49,920 --> 00:12:52,080
like there's a lot more people working as drivers now

333
00:12:52,080 --> 00:12:53,320
than there were 10 years ago.

334
00:12:53,320 --> 00:12:54,040
I could be wrong.

335
00:12:54,040 --> 00:12:57,400
That's my impression.

336
00:12:57,400 --> 00:12:59,880
And then the last big cluster of area

337
00:12:59,880 --> 00:13:02,560
there that I talk about, pessimistic bias.

338
00:13:02,560 --> 00:13:04,160
Compared to economists, non-economists

339
00:13:04,160 --> 00:13:06,840
tend to see an economy in decline, bad now,

340
00:13:06,840 --> 00:13:09,080
and going to get even worse in the future.

341
00:13:09,080 --> 00:13:13,440
This is the relative difference in views.

342
00:13:13,440 --> 00:13:17,120
This is also the one that seems more modern.

343
00:13:17,120 --> 00:13:20,520
There are some incident among 18th and 19th century economists

344
00:13:20,520 --> 00:13:22,560
complaining about the pessimism of the public.

345
00:13:22,560 --> 00:13:24,480
But there's actually also plenty of economists

346
00:13:24,480 --> 00:13:28,560
in those centuries talking about the ridiculous optimism

347
00:13:28,560 --> 00:13:31,240
of their opponents, especially making fun

348
00:13:31,240 --> 00:13:33,920
of the optimism of socialists.

349
00:13:33,920 --> 00:13:35,640
So like the iron law of wages, the view

350
00:13:35,640 --> 00:13:37,720
that wages are going to be suckers of subsistence

351
00:13:37,720 --> 00:13:39,360
for regular workers forever.

352
00:13:39,360 --> 00:13:42,280
Samathas and Ricardo.

353
00:13:42,280 --> 00:13:44,560
And they've got a story saying, well,

354
00:13:44,560 --> 00:13:45,840
once wages get above subsistence,

355
00:13:45,840 --> 00:13:47,500
the workers will just breed like rabbits

356
00:13:47,500 --> 00:13:49,400
and that will bring wages back down.

357
00:13:49,400 --> 00:13:53,120
Whereas it was actually some very,

358
00:13:53,120 --> 00:13:54,200
well, what's the right way?

359
00:13:54,200 --> 00:13:55,960
Socialists who sound like they're on drugs

360
00:13:55,960 --> 00:13:58,240
when you read them, who actually are

361
00:13:58,240 --> 00:14:00,600
accurate in predicting an amazing future

362
00:14:00,600 --> 00:14:03,640
of fantastic prosperity for the common man.

363
00:14:03,640 --> 00:14:05,720
They're wrong in how it would occur.

364
00:14:05,720 --> 00:14:08,040
But if you read someone like Fourier predicting

365
00:14:08,040 --> 00:14:10,720
oceans full of lemonade, that's more accurate

366
00:14:10,720 --> 00:14:14,680
than Malthus, just a planet perpetually

367
00:14:14,680 --> 00:14:17,040
at the level of starvation.

368
00:14:17,040 --> 00:14:19,560
We don't have, the ocean is not literally full of lemonades,

369
00:14:19,560 --> 00:14:22,240
but there are unlimited amounts of lemonade available

370
00:14:22,240 --> 00:14:22,840
dirt cheap.

371
00:14:22,840 --> 00:14:27,040
So for practical purposes, we do have oceans of lemonade.

372
00:14:27,040 --> 00:14:30,760
So Fourier was more right than Malthus on stuff like this.

373
00:14:30,760 --> 00:14:35,080
But once we get into the second half of the 20th century,

374
00:14:35,080 --> 00:14:37,320
then it does seem like economists

375
00:14:37,320 --> 00:14:39,560
are a voice for and hot for optimism

376
00:14:39,560 --> 00:14:41,240
compared to the general public, which

377
00:14:41,240 --> 00:14:43,960
is very inclined to think, we've run out of new ideas,

378
00:14:43,960 --> 00:14:46,860
or we're going to have permanent 30% unemployment.

379
00:14:46,860 --> 00:14:49,440
Or the environment is going to be destroyed,

380
00:14:49,440 --> 00:14:51,760
and we're going to run out of natural resources,

381
00:14:51,760 --> 00:14:53,280
we're going to starve.

382
00:14:53,280 --> 00:14:57,640
So probably more, let's see, maybe

383
00:14:57,640 --> 00:14:59,920
that a lot of this is connected to the environmentalist

384
00:14:59,920 --> 00:15:04,360
movements, but there's other stuff going on too.

385
00:15:04,360 --> 00:15:06,440
Now in terms of what kinds of bad policies

386
00:15:06,440 --> 00:15:08,520
you get as a result of this bias, if it is indeed,

387
00:15:08,520 --> 00:15:10,080
as I say, bias.

388
00:15:10,080 --> 00:15:12,480
So it's not as clear as for the other three.

389
00:15:12,480 --> 00:15:14,840
Someone just says, oh my god, everything's terrible

390
00:15:14,840 --> 00:15:16,800
and it's going to get worse.

391
00:15:16,800 --> 00:15:19,360
And therefore, what should we do?

392
00:15:19,360 --> 00:15:21,840
You might just say, we're doomed.

393
00:15:21,840 --> 00:15:24,040
No point.

394
00:15:24,040 --> 00:15:28,120
So it's not that clear logically why just being a pessimist

395
00:15:28,120 --> 00:15:31,200
would incline you to favor any bringing new policies.

396
00:15:31,200 --> 00:15:32,760
What I will say is based upon what

397
00:15:32,760 --> 00:15:35,360
I call correlational bumper sticker analysis.

398
00:15:35,360 --> 00:15:37,680
I'm just looking, what kinds of, like if you

399
00:15:37,680 --> 00:15:39,960
look at cars with pessimistic bumper stickers,

400
00:15:39,960 --> 00:15:42,800
what kinds of other bumper stickers do they usually have?

401
00:15:42,800 --> 00:15:44,840
And it does seem like in almost all countries

402
00:15:44,840 --> 00:15:47,040
that I've been in that there's a left wing pattern.

403
00:15:47,040 --> 00:15:50,240
So people who just say that the economy and prosperity

404
00:15:50,240 --> 00:15:52,080
and the future and the planet are doomed also

405
00:15:52,080 --> 00:15:54,480
tend to have left wing bumper stickers on them.

406
00:15:54,480 --> 00:15:57,120
Not entirely, but it seems like that is the pattern.

407
00:15:57,120 --> 00:15:59,000
When I was in Sweden, I asked them,

408
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:00,840
so here when someone says everything

409
00:16:00,840 --> 00:16:02,920
is terrible here in Sweden, is that

410
00:16:02,920 --> 00:16:06,280
taken as a thinly veiled criticism

411
00:16:06,280 --> 00:16:10,480
of Swedish social democracy or as a thinly veiled criticism

412
00:16:10,480 --> 00:16:12,760
of free market capitalism?

413
00:16:12,760 --> 00:16:15,320
And the Swedish answer was it's a criticism

414
00:16:15,320 --> 00:16:17,040
of free market capitalism.

415
00:16:17,040 --> 00:16:18,680
So even in Sweden, when they have

416
00:16:18,680 --> 00:16:21,880
a whole ideology of the third way, the people's home,

417
00:16:21,880 --> 00:16:24,000
still anyone who complains, people

418
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:26,960
think you're pointing a finger at free markets.

419
00:16:26,960 --> 00:16:31,880
So is there any place where being just a big complainer

420
00:16:31,880 --> 00:16:37,000
is, does seem to be a call for smaller governments?

421
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:40,200
So I will say the Soviet Union in the 1980s.

422
00:16:40,200 --> 00:16:42,400
So there when people said this is living hell,

423
00:16:42,400 --> 00:16:44,640
this is a terrible country, that's

424
00:16:44,640 --> 00:16:47,640
the kind of thing that gets you thrown into Siberia.

425
00:16:47,640 --> 00:16:50,560
You don't want to be in the workers' paradise

426
00:16:50,560 --> 00:16:52,880
saying everything is terrible.

427
00:16:52,880 --> 00:16:57,400
And when people in the 1980s started saying this,

428
00:16:57,400 --> 00:17:01,120
it was taken as a thinly veiled call for free market reforms

429
00:17:01,120 --> 00:17:05,040
and just to say that socialism is a terrible rock system.

430
00:17:05,040 --> 00:17:09,280
But it's striking how socialist you have to be before being

431
00:17:09,280 --> 00:17:14,280
a miserable complainer is a call for free markets.

432
00:17:14,280 --> 00:17:17,560
Does seem like you actually need to be a communism or something

433
00:17:17,560 --> 00:17:19,960
close to that.

434
00:17:19,960 --> 00:17:24,120
So I think a lot more needs to be done on this.

435
00:17:24,120 --> 00:17:25,320
This is just my impression.

436
00:17:25,320 --> 00:17:27,480
But it could be wrong or there could be more to it.

437
00:17:27,480 --> 00:17:27,960
Yes?

438
00:17:27,960 --> 00:17:30,160
The result of higher time preference policies maybe?

439
00:17:30,160 --> 00:17:32,160
Is everything going to be terrible in the future

440
00:17:32,160 --> 00:17:33,720
if it's not worth saving for?

441
00:17:33,720 --> 00:17:35,720
Maybe, although remember there's an income and a substitution

442
00:17:35,720 --> 00:17:36,240
effect.

443
00:17:36,240 --> 00:17:37,520
So you might say if everything's going

444
00:17:37,520 --> 00:17:39,860
to be terrible in the future, we better start saving now

445
00:17:39,860 --> 00:17:41,360
so we're ready for doomsday.

446
00:17:41,360 --> 00:17:44,320
What does survivalists do?

447
00:17:44,320 --> 00:17:45,640
Survivalists are people who think

448
00:17:45,640 --> 00:17:48,280
the future is going to be terrible and so they go

449
00:17:48,280 --> 00:17:48,920
and they prepare.

450
00:17:52,480 --> 00:17:56,160
I'd be curious, are there people who

451
00:17:56,160 --> 00:17:58,200
were going to be survivalists and then they said,

452
00:17:58,200 --> 00:18:00,600
no, things are so terrible, there's no point.

453
00:18:00,600 --> 00:18:02,680
Might as well live it up.

454
00:18:02,680 --> 00:18:06,480
So when there's a really horrible war about to happen,

455
00:18:06,480 --> 00:18:08,360
then it does seem like you see this high time preference

456
00:18:08,360 --> 00:18:08,860
behavior.

457
00:18:08,860 --> 00:18:10,800
But then again, maybe that's what you see.

458
00:18:10,800 --> 00:18:12,560
There's also probably a bunch of other people

459
00:18:12,560 --> 00:18:15,520
that aren't in public going and buying a pint of champagne

460
00:18:15,520 --> 00:18:18,160
who are trying to go and hide some food for a tough time.

461
00:18:18,160 --> 00:18:21,640
So maybe there's just a visibility effect.

462
00:18:21,640 --> 00:18:23,200
Yeah, Nathan?

463
00:18:23,200 --> 00:18:27,000
So I want to comment on the question.

464
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:29,240
So comment, it seems like in terms

465
00:18:29,240 --> 00:18:32,600
of right wing examples of pessimistic bias,

466
00:18:32,600 --> 00:18:35,080
make America great again implies that it's gotten worse.

467
00:18:35,080 --> 00:18:38,640
But that's also an anti-market form of right wing ideology.

468
00:18:38,640 --> 00:18:40,360
Right, right, yes.

469
00:18:40,360 --> 00:18:42,720
I mean, it's primarily anti-foreign rather than

470
00:18:42,720 --> 00:18:43,720
anti-market.

471
00:18:43,720 --> 00:18:45,520
It's not saying I hate American business.

472
00:18:45,520 --> 00:18:47,320
I'm saying American business would

473
00:18:47,320 --> 00:18:49,520
win if it weren't for these cheating foreigners who

474
00:18:49,520 --> 00:18:51,200
are ripping us off and gaming the system

475
00:18:51,200 --> 00:18:54,200
and manipulating their currencies and paying slave

476
00:18:54,200 --> 00:18:56,960
wages and putting lead in their products.

477
00:19:00,120 --> 00:19:02,360
So I guess you might say in recent years

478
00:19:02,360 --> 00:19:05,360
that there has been, to some degree, mainstreaming

479
00:19:05,360 --> 00:19:08,640
of racial pessimism and demographic pessimism,

480
00:19:08,640 --> 00:19:11,240
thinking that there's going to be

481
00:19:11,240 --> 00:19:13,800
some kind of top-terrible racial collapse or something

482
00:19:13,800 --> 00:19:14,320
like that.

483
00:19:14,320 --> 00:19:17,000
So I say that's still pretty fringe.

484
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:19,720
It's a very loud, small number of very loud people.

485
00:19:19,720 --> 00:19:22,800
But it's not clear they really speak for any other people.

486
00:19:22,800 --> 00:19:23,300
Yes?

487
00:19:23,300 --> 00:19:28,560
So one of the recent, to refer to the make work bias,

488
00:19:28,560 --> 00:19:31,040
and one of the recent news things that came out

489
00:19:31,040 --> 00:19:33,440
was Bill Gates and the robot tax.

490
00:19:33,440 --> 00:19:34,880
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yes, yes.

491
00:19:34,880 --> 00:19:37,360
And I'm just, what do you think is the political economy

492
00:19:37,360 --> 00:19:37,860
of that?

493
00:19:37,860 --> 00:19:39,560
Do you think that, because how is someone

494
00:19:39,560 --> 00:19:41,160
as smart as Bill Gates going to put something

495
00:19:41,160 --> 00:19:42,360
as stupid as that forward?

496
00:19:42,360 --> 00:19:42,880
Yeah.

497
00:19:42,880 --> 00:19:44,680
But is there like a Boone-Lakers-Batkins?

498
00:19:44,680 --> 00:19:45,720
Albert Einstein was a socialist.

499
00:19:45,720 --> 00:19:46,220
What's that?

500
00:19:46,220 --> 00:19:47,600
Albert Einstein was a socialist.

501
00:19:47,600 --> 00:19:48,800
That was just the one thing.

502
00:19:48,800 --> 00:19:49,600
There's a whirlwind of intelligence

503
00:19:49,600 --> 00:19:50,920
and other things that I've seen.

504
00:19:50,920 --> 00:19:51,420
Yeah.

505
00:19:51,420 --> 00:19:53,400
So what I'd say is, so smarter people generally

506
00:19:53,400 --> 00:19:55,960
do have less bad views, as we're going to see in a minute.

507
00:19:55,960 --> 00:19:58,720
But still, they can be very bad.

508
00:19:58,720 --> 00:20:01,280
I'm just curious if there could be.


